            <ArticleTitle>[Relationship between the assessment of coronary risk using the Italian chart and the assessment with the New Zealand chart].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the distribution of coronary risk in primary prevention using both the New Zealand and Italian charts, and to assess whether there is a relationship between the two scales built on data from populations at different natural coronary risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Carpi's district, Territorial Health Authority in Modena province.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Population between 40 and 69 years old without any background of cardiovascular disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The variables studied corresponded to the Jackson's New Zealand table and the Italian Progetto Cuore's one. Concordance was assessed with the kappa coefficient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We studied 1850 men and 2235 women with an average age of 56 years. The percentage of 'high' coronary risk men and women at the New Zealand's chart was 27% and 8% respectively. Ten percent of 'high' risk men and no woman were found at the Italian chart. The kappa coefficient to evaluate the agreement on high risk men between the two charts was 'moderate' (kappa = 0.403). No estimation was allowed for women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There was moderate agreement between the New Zealand and the Progetto Cuore charts.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
